<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vector-Valued Functions and Motion in Space</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we shift our focus from static geometric objects to curves and motion in space.
    Vector-valued functions provide a natural way to describe paths traced by particles, where position,
    velocity, and acceleration are all represented using vectors. This perspective allows us to study
    motion geometrically while working componentwise in calculations.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by defining vector-valued functions and examining their limits, continuity, and derivatives.
    These ideas extend familiar concepts from single-variable calculus, but now apply to curves in two
    and three dimensions. Building on derivatives, we introduce velocity and acceleration vectors and
    use them to analyze how a particle moves along a curve.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter then develops a geometric description of motion using tangent, normal, and binormal
    vectors, arc length, and curvature. These tools help describe not just where a particle is, but how
    its path bends and how its direction changes. By the end of the chapter, you should be able to
    connect algebraic computations to the geometric behavior of curves in space, laying the groundwork
    for later topics in vector calculus.
  </p>
</introduction>


